# Credentials never live in the repo. UDL_USER and UDL_PASS are environment
# variables supplied by the App Store app configuration.
#
# Targeted rather than a blanket *.ini. The blanket rule silently swallowed
# pytest.ini, so the upload artefact carried no coverage configuration, the
# platform's pytest run emitted no coverage.xml, and the SonarQube gate reads
# the artefact rather than the suite: a 99 per cent covered codebase would have
# scored zero. Keep this list specific.
credentials.ini
secrets.ini
*credentials*.ini
!pytest.ini

# Local reference material, not part of the upload artefact
reference/

# Python
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.coverage

# coverage.xml is TRACKED, and so is coverage-reports/ below.
#
# The code-quality stage is a separate job from the test stage and starts from
# a fresh checkout ("Initialized empty Git repository ... Created fresh
# repository"). A report written by the test run is not in that checkout unless
# the pipeline carries it across, and the evidence says it does not: a real
# SonarQube scanning this tree with no report present scores exactly 0.0% and
# logs "No report was found for sonar.python.coverage.reportPaths", which is
# the gate's result and its wording. The same tree WITH the report scores
# 100.0%. See docker/sonar-probe.sh, which runs that experiment.
#
# So the report is committed, because that is the only way it can be in a
# checkout we do not control. This is a workaround for a pipeline we cannot
# see, not a habit: a generated file in a repository goes stale and then lies.
# tests/test_coverage_report.py fails if the committed report does not match
# what the suite currently produces, so staleness is a test failure rather than
# a quiet misstatement. If the platform confirms it carries the report between
# jobs, stop tracking both and ignore them again.
htmlcov/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# Local run output
/data/
runs/
phase_offset_*.html

# coverage-reports/ is TRACKED too, for the reason above. It holds the same
# report at coverage-reports/coverage-timeslides.xml, which is where the
# SonarQube Python plugin looks when no reportPaths is configured: its default
# is coverage-reports/*coverage-*.xml. Proven, not assumed -- scanning without
# sonar-project.properties, the scanner logs
# "Parsing report '/usr/src/coverage-reports/coverage-timeslides.xml'".
